I've instructed the Commissioner of  Parks and Recreation to issue  regulations that would ban outdoor  feeding in all city parks earlier this  year Philadelphia the City of Brotherly  Love passed a new law making it illegal  to feed the homeless in public joining  New York Las Vegas Orlando and other  cities that have tried to enact similar  bans so are these new rules really  helping the homeless brian jenkins leads  a nonprofit called chosen 300 which  among other things gives food to  homeless people on the street he says  that while ideally the homeless would go  to soup kitchens for all their meals  that's simply not realistic there's  people who are not able to go indoors  because if they go into enclosed spaces  with a lot of people they can cause  fights they cannot cause harm to  themselves and also harm to others so  we're able to reach those folks so that  way they can sit in the park by  themselves not being bothered by anybody  but they can get the right to eat and  that's so important because if they  don't get the right to eat then they're  gonna do whatever they have to do to get  something to eat without a hint of irony  Philadelphia Mayor Michael Nutter said  that when charities give homeless people  fresh food on the street there's a risk  of food poisoning because food served  outdoors often isn't handled properly he  also argues that often the real problem  is mental illness as the mayor told a  federal judge they're not just hungry  they have other needs jenkins says that  sometimes feeding homeless people  outdoors is the best way to address  those other needs we're not just giving  them the meal we're giving them our  affection our cares are love and sharing  hope with them and then also referring  them to services that are so valuable to  them to move them towards a state of  self-sufficiency so what's really behind  these new laws making Philadelphia look  pretty for visitors the ACLU which sued  and won a temporary injunction against  the new law says the real reason for the  ban is protecting the city's image as a  tourist destination since the biggest  gathering spot for Philly's homeless  population is adjacent to its  world-renowned art museums these laws  are designed to really eliminate people  out of plain view so that we can have  this idea that the city doesn't have a  homeless problem you have to solve the  problem and not just move the problem  the federal lawsuit will come to trial  early next year in both the city and  Bryan  Jenkins group are working to resolve the  standoff out-of-court whether that  settlement will allow voluntary  organizations to do what they think is  best  remains to be seen for reason TV I'm  Nicholas  [Music]
 

